\description{
frmtmb registers the methods that marginaleffects needs:
\code{get_coef()}, \code{set_coef()}, \code{get_vcov()} and \code{get_predict()}. So
\code{predictions()}, \code{comparisons()}, \code{slopes()}, \code{hypotheses()} and
their \code{avg_} forms work on a \code{frmtmb_fit}. The standard errors come
from the delta method, which marginaleffects applies with a
numerical Jacobian.
}
\section{The parameters marginaleffects perturbs}{

\code{get_coef()} gives every estimated coefficient, then the parameters
that a prediction reads outside them: the thresholds and \code{cs()}
coefficients of an ordinal model, and the free coordinates of each
\code{mo()} simplex. If a \code{mo()} term has a category that no row is in,
the data do not determine its simplex. Then \code{get_coef()} gives the
cumulative weights at the categories that rows are in
(\verb{zeta<j>_cum<c>}), which the data determine. A prediction at an
empty category gets a standard error of \code{NaN}, with one warning per
fit, as \code{fitted()} gives it. The other standard errors stay finite.
A \code{mo()} term on one value is 0 on every row, so no prediction reads
its coefficient, and that coefficient gets a variance of 0 in
\code{get_vcov()}.

A prediction that keeps the group-level effects is conditional on
their estimated modes, as for lme4 and glmmTMB fits.
}

\section{The vcov argument}{

marginaleffects passes its \code{vcov} argument to \code{get_vcov()}:
\describe{
\item{\code{TRUE} or \code{NULL}}{The model-based covariance (the default).}
\item{\code{FALSE}}{No standard errors.}
\item{A matrix}{A covariance over \code{get_coef()}, matched by its
names. An unnamed matrix is read in the order of \code{get_coef()},
with marginaleffects' warning. A matrix over the whole outer
parameter vector, as \code{vcov(fit, full = TRUE)} and
\code{vcov_cluster(full = TRUE)} give it, is cut down to the rows of
\code{get_coef()}. A REML fit has no fixed effects in that vector, so
such a matrix is refused there. If the matrix has no finite
value in a row of \code{get_coef()}, as \code{vcov(fit, full = TRUE)} has
for a parameter the fit does not determine, a warning names the
row: every standard error is then \code{NaN}.}
\item{A function}{A function of the fit that returns one of these
matrices.}
\item{\code{"HC0"}}{The sandwich with each row as its own cluster
(\code{vcov_cluster(type = "CR0")}).}
\item{\code{"HC1"} or \code{"stata"}}{HC0 times \code{n / (n - k)}, as for
\code{lm()}, with \code{k} the number of population-level coefficients
(those of every linear predictor but the distributional
parameters') plus the steps of each \code{mo()} simplex that the data
determine; a \code{mo()} term on one value counts nothing, as \code{lm()}
does not count a column it aliases. So \code{k} is the column count of
\code{lm()} on \code{factor()} of the \code{mo()} variable. marginaleffects maps
\code{"stata"} to HC1 for \code{lm()}.}
\item{A one-sided formula, \code{~ g}}{The cluster sandwich over \code{g},
\code{vcov_cluster(type = "CR1")}, with the factor \code{G / (G - 1)}.}
}
The bread of these sandwiches is the inverse of the observed
information. \code{sandwich::vcovHC()} and \code{vcovCL()} use the expected
information of a glm. The two are equal for a gaussian model and for
a glm with its canonical link, so there \code{"HC0"} equals
\code{vcovHC(type = "HC0")}, and \code{~ g} equals \code{vcovCL()} for a glm (for
\code{lm()}, \code{vcovCL()} also multiplies by \code{(n - 1) / (n - k)}). For
another link they differ: on 300 rows of a \code{Gamma(link = "log")}
model, the standard error of the slope is 1.128 times
\code{vcovHC()}'s. The sandwiches are formed on the directions the data
determine, so they stay finite where the default covariance does: on
a fit with an empty \code{mo()} category, a \code{mo()} term on one value or a
variance at its boundary.

The other strings that marginaleffects knows are refused, each with
its reason. \code{"HC2"} to \code{"HC5"} and \code{"robust"} (which
marginaleffects maps to HC3) weight each row by its leverage, the
diagonal of a hat matrix, which frmtmb does not compute. \code{"HAC"},
\code{"NeweyWest"} and \code{"kernHAC"} need a time order of the rows.
\code{"satterthwaite"} and \code{"kenward-roger"} are denominator degrees of
freedom for a linear mixed model fitted by REML. \code{vcov_cluster()}
refuses a fit whose random effects cross the clusters, and so
\code{"HC0"} and \code{"HC1"} refuse such a fit too. It also refuses a
residual correlation term (\code{ar()}, \code{ma()} or \code{arma()}, either form,
and the other residual covariance terms), whose density is joint over
each group: under one, \code{"HC0"}, \code{"HC1"}, \code{"stata"} and a cluster
formula are refused, \code{vcov = TRUE} gives the model-based standard
errors, and \code{\link[=frm_bootstrap]{frm_bootstrap()}} is the route to a robust one.
}

\section{What the standard error holds}{

marginaleffects forms a standard error from the coefficients \code{get_coef()}
holds, the population-level and distributional ones, by the delta method. It
cannot carry a variance that is in no such coefficient. The default \code{vcov}
gives the warning of class \code{frmtmb_interop_se_part} on a fit where that gap
exists, because \code{get_vcov()} is not told which rows or which quantity it
serves.
\itemize{
\item A \code{me()} term. Every row reads as a new row, whose latent value brms draws
from \code{N(x, sdx)}. The standard error of a level reads 0.07 where
\code{\link[=frm_linpred]{frm_linpred()}} reads 1.11. A slope, a contrast or a test of a coefficient
needs no such draw and is right: -0.491, standard error 0.0485.
\item A smooth, \code{gp()} or \code{hsgp()} term. \code{get_coef()} holds no coefficient of the
field. On a \code{gp()} the standard error is 0.31 on every row, where
\code{\link[=frm_linpred]{frm_linpred()}} reads 0.087 to 0.695; on \code{s(x)} it reads 0.70, 0.12, 1.07,
1.59 where \code{\link[=frm_linpred]{frm_linpred()}} reads 0.088, 0.068, 0.55, 1.32. It is too wide
near the data and too narrow far from it.
\item Group-level effects, with \code{re_formula} keeping them. The standard error
holds neither their uncertainty nor their covariance with the intercept:
0.209 where \code{\link[=fitted.frmtmb_fit]{fitted.frmtmb_fit()}} reads 0.104 on a gaussian \code{(1 | g)},
1.435 where it reads 0.488 on a poisson one. Under \code{re_formula = NA} the
two agree, and no warning comes.
}

\code{frm_linpred(se.fit = TRUE)} and \code{\link[=fitted.frmtmb_fit]{fitted.frmtmb_fit()}} carry each of these.
On the response scale of a \code{me()} fit, the estimate is the mean over the
draw, as \code{\link[=fitted.frmtmb_fit]{fitted.frmtmb_fit()}} reads it. \code{vcov = FALSE} gives the estimates
alone. A covariance matrix passed as \code{vcov} is read as you give it, and gives
no warning.
}

\section{brms's prediction arguments}{

\code{re_formula}, \code{allow_new_levels}, \code{dpar} (also under brms's name
\code{nlpar}) and \code{resp} go to \code{\link[=frm_linpred]{frm_linpred()}}, as they go to brms's
prediction for a \code{brmsfit}. \code{re_formula = NA} or \code{~ 0} gives the
population-level prediction, and \code{NULL} (the default) keeps every
group-level effect. The arguments that marginaleffects passes for
other model classes and frmtmb does not take are refused by name:
lme4's \code{re.form} and \code{allow.new.levels}, brms's \code{sample_new_levels},
\code{ndraws}, \code{draw_ids} and \code{incl_autocor}, and glmmTMB's \code{zitype}.
marginaleffects 1.0.0 keeps a fixed
list of the arguments that it knows for each model class, and a
package cannot add to that list. So marginaleffects warns that
\code{re_formula} is "not known to be supported" for a \code{frmtmb_fit}, and
then passes it on. The argument has its effect.
}

\examples{
\donttest{
if (requireNamespace("marginaleffects", quietly = TRUE)) {
  set.seed(1)
  d <- data.frame(x = rnorm(120), g = factor(rep(1:12, each = 10)))
  d$y <- 1 + 0.5 * d$x + rnorm(12)[d$g] + rnorm(120)
  fit <- frm(y ~ x + (1 | g), data = d)
  nd <- data.frame(x = c(-1, 1), g = factor(1, levels = 1:12))
  # population-level predictions (marginaleffects warns, see above)
  suppressWarnings(marginaleffects::predictions(fit, newdata = nd,
                                                re_formula = NA))
  # a cluster-robust covariance
  fit0 <- frm(y ~ x, data = d)
  marginaleffects::avg_slopes(fit0, variables = "x", vcov = ~ g)
}
}
